  "problem": "Let $P={{z}_{0},{z}_{1},\u2026,{z}_{q}}$ be a partition of the interval $[-7,-1].$ If $\u0394{z}_{k}={z}_{k}-{z}_{k-1},$ calculate ${\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}\u0394{z}_{k}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Note that ${\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}\u0394{z}_{k}$ is just the length of the interval $[-7,-1],$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}\u0394{z}_{k} & =({z}_{1}-{z}_{0})+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})+({z}_{3}-{z}_{2})+\u22ef+({z}_{q}-{z}_{q-1}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{z}_{1}}-{z}_{0})+(\\cancel{{z}_{2}}-\\cancel{{z}_{1}})+(\\cancel{{z}_{3}}-\\cancel{{z}_{2}})+\u22ef+({z}_{q}-\\cancel{{z}_{q-1}}) \\\\  & =-{z}_{0}+{z}_{q} \\\\  & ={z}_{q}-{z}_{0} \\\\  & =(-1)-(-7) \\\\  & =7-1 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
